{
  "id": "Compound::DB01583 - Gene::7276",
  "verbalization": "Liotrix is implicated in the pathogenesis of multiple skeletal disorders, including Craniosynostosis, Slipped Femoral Capital Epiphysis, and Epiphysiolysis, all of which are linked to Levothyroxine. These conditions are characterized by abnormal bone development, and Levothyroxine is known to bind to Ttr, a key component in the thyroid hormone metabolism pathway. The shared mechanism of action between Liotrix and Levothyroxine, both of which bind to Ttr, suggests a potential functional overlap in their effects on the thyroid-related biological processes. The presence of a common ancestor, Thyroid Agent, and the shared pathway involving Phenylalanine Derivative further supports a unified regulatory framework. The integration of these findings implies that Liotrix may exert its effects through a mechanism that involves Ttr, potentially by modulating thyroid hormone signaling, thereby linking the drug to the molecular pathways associated with Ttr.",
